\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Reasoning models such as DeepSeek-R1~\cite{deepseekr1} and QwQ~\cite{qwq} scale inference-time computation through extended chains of thought (CoT). Recent work documents that these models can ``self-jailbreak'': without adversarial input modification, the CoT transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation~\cite{harmthoughts,selfjailbreak}. We define the first reasoning step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases as the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP). This failure mode differs qualitatively from adversarial jailbreaks on standard LLMs because the harmful transition originates within the model's own reasoning. Recent work shows that chain-of-thought faithfulness is limited~\citep{chen2025unfaithful}, motivating hidden-state monitoring that bypasses surface text. Before retrospective characterization can inform future detection systems, a prior question must be answered: can current probing methods reliably distinguish pre-CP from post-CP reasoning steps, and under what conditions? This paper provides \emph{retrospective} classification (not online detection) of what hidden-state and text-based probes can and cannot detect; false positive rates remain 11--50\%, so deployment applications are future work.\looseness=-1

Existing safety detectors target fundamentally different problems. Hidden-state (HS) methods such as TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} and SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} classify adversarial inputs at the token level, while text monitors like SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} scan generated reasoning for explicit violations. Neither tracks the gradual, multi-step commitment process. HS probes require choosing which transformer layer to monitor, a researcher degree of freedom typically treated as a minor implementation detail. Before asking which modality is better, a prior methodological question must be answered: how sensitive are probe conclusions to the choice of layer selection metric? We show this choice alone can reverse a study's central conclusion: the same data yield a 14-layer (R1-8B) to 61-layer (QwQ-32B) divergence depending solely on metric choice; for QwQ-32B, at threshold-based layers the HS advantage is +19pp ($p{<}0.005$); at crossing-rate-selected layers, it is non-significant ($p{=}0.83$). Prior hidden-state probing targets standard LLMs~\citep{zhou2024alignment,arditi2024refusal}; prior reasoning safety work remains at the text level~\citep{harmthoughts,safethink}. The intersection---hidden-state monitoring of reasoning model safety trajectories---is unexplored, and existing hidden-state analyses operate across layers (spatial) rather than across generation steps (temporal). \cref{fig:concept_overview} illustrates this metric-induced reversal.\looseness=-1

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/fig_concept_overview.pdf}
\caption{Metric-induced layer selection reversal. The same hidden states, evaluated with different layer selection metrics, yield opposite conclusions about the HS advantage. Crossing-rate composite selects shallow layers (L0--L10), where HS and text probes perform equivalently ($p{=}0.83$); threshold-based metrics select deep layers (L14--L63), where HS probes significantly outperform text (+20pp, $p{<}0.005$). The 14--61 layer gap arises from the metric alone.}
\label{fig:concept_overview}
\end{figure*}

We conduct a systematic analysis across four reasoning models, primarily Qwen-based (3/4 models), with one Llama model providing evidence from a second architecture family, at two scales (7--8B, 32B) on the HarmThoughts benchmark. Our central finding is methodological: the choice of layer selection metric entirely determines whether an HS advantage is observed. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) selects shallow layers for 32B models because shallow representations compress directional information, so a linear probe operates near the noise floor and threshold crossings reflect noise rather than semantic transitions. At these layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage.\looseness=-1

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Arch.} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{Hidden} & \textbf{$n$ (total\,/\,test)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & Llama & 8B & 32 & 4096 & 209\,/\,43 \\
OT-7B & Qwen & 7B & 28 & 3584 & 228\,/\,47 \\
R1-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 100\,/\,20 \\
QwQ-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 95\,/\,19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Models studied. R1-8B and R1-32B are distilled from DeepSeek-R1~\citep{deepseekr1}; QwQ-32B~\citep{qwq} is a native reasoning model; OT-7B~\citep{openthinker} is trained with STILL~\citep{still} on Qwen2.5-7B. Data: HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts} with step-level CP annotations (CP = first reasoning step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases). Split: 60/20/20.}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

Threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) share a common confusion matrix and empirically converge; BA independently confirms deep-layer selection (\cref{tab:layer_selection}). At these layers, the HS advantage is graded: \emph{validated via nested 5-fold CV} at 7--8B (precision +20.1 to +21.9pp; \cref{tab:hs_vs_text}), \emph{significant} but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ (+19.2pp; \cref{sec:32b-analysis}), and \emph{exploratory} at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (+15.4pp, pattern consistent with position mediation, below MDE; \cref{sec:experiments}).\looseness=-1

Our contributions are threefold. \textbf{(1)~Metric-induced layer selection bias}: CR-composite metrics systematically select shallow layers across all four models, both datasets (HarmThoughts, AdvBench; \cref{app:advbench_layer_bias}), and extend to Gemma-2 (\cref{app:gemma_cross_arch}), producing a qualitative reversal ($p{=}0.83$ vs.\ $p{<}0.005$) from metric choice alone; random-label controls confirm genuine safety labels are required (\cref{sec:layer-results}). While metric sensitivity in probing is well-documented~\citep{belinkov2022probing,voita2020information}, we identify a novel \emph{cascade}---metric choice determines layer, which determines conclusion---whose severity (14--61 layer divergence, qualitative sign reversal) has not, to our knowledge, been documented in the probing literature. \textbf{(2)~Boundary conditions}: the HS advantage is encoder-conditional (diminishes from 4/4 to 1/4 HB with stronger encoders; PCA capacity matching preserves significance) and partially overlaps with position (3/4 linear, 2/4 quadratic; \cref{sec:32b-analysis}). \textbf{(3)~Negative results}: no alternative method matches supervised probing (\cref{tab:negative}); control task selectivity (11--17pp above chance; \cref{app:control}) confirms genuine safety structure. These findings establish that layer selection metric is a critical specification choice in hidden-state safety probing---one severe enough to qualitatively reverse study conclusions.\looseness=-1

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\paragraph{Evidence gradient.} We classify HS advantage evidence into three tiers: \emph{cross-validated}\footnote{In our usage, \emph{cross-validated} means the result survives nested 5-fold cross-validation with a held-out test fold, ruling out hyperparameter overfitting. \emph{Exploratory} ($\dagger$) results pass HB on the holdout split but lack nested CV validation due to sample constraints.} (nested CV pass; 7--8B models), \emph{significant} (HB pass without nested CV; QwQ-32B), and \emph{exploratory} ($^\dagger$, small sample or narrow coverage; R1-32B). Per-model details follow in \cref{sec:layer-results,sec:hs-text,sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B, trained on the OpenThoughts dataset~\citep{openthinker}; Qwen-7B backbone, STILL-trained~\cref{app:ot7b_details}, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Classification requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings (\cref{tab:k_ablation}). Statistical testing follows \cref{sec:evaluation}.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the CR-composite systematically selects shallow layers: L0 for R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep; \cref{tab:r18b_full_layer}) and OT-7B, L2 (3\%) for QwQ-32B, L10 (16\%) for R1-32B (\cref{fig:layer_heatmap}). Threshold-based metrics converge to deep layers---L63 (98\%) for both 32B models. BA varies across splits (7--98\% depth; \cref{app:ba_layer_comparison}). The mechanism is cosine compression: step-to-step distances are 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller at shallow layers (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), so a linear probe operates near the noise floor, inflating crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).\looseness=-1

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{CR-comp.}$^a$ & \textbf{Thresh.}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(thresh.)} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec}$^c$ \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L0 (0\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +20.1 \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +21.9 \\
QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ & L2 (3\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.005$ & +19.2 \\
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +15.4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,Crossing-rate composite (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate). $^b$\,Consensus of threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite). $^c$\,HS$-$text precision difference (pp) at threshold-based layer (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). Rightmost $p$: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at threshold-based layer (e.g., QwQ-32B CR-composite layer: $p{=}0.83$, non-significant). $\dagger$\,Sample-limited ($n \leq 20$).}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_layer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer metric heatmap (2$\times$2). Each column: one of five metrics (balanced accuracy, precision, inverse FPR, crossing rate, additive composite; min-max normalized). Red dashed: CR-composite layer (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate); green dashed: threshold-based layer (consensus of threshold-based metrics). For R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep), crossing rate drives CR-composite selection to L0 while threshold-based metrics retain L14. At 32B, CR-composite selections cluster near input layers while threshold-based metrics peak at deep layers.}
\label{fig:layer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The downstream impact is severe: at CR-composite layers, only 7--8B models show significant HS advantage; both 32B models fail. CR-composite shallow-layer selection is an artifact of cosine compression, not a genuine safety signal; random-label controls confirm this (\cref{app:random_cp}). At threshold-based layers, the HS advantage is graded: cross-validated at 7--8B, significant but position-competitive at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$, and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (below MDE). $\dagger$ marks sample-limited tests ($n \leq 20$). Random-label controls confirm threshold-based selection requires genuine safety labels; the geometric bias is Qwen-specific (\cref{app:random_cp}). AUROC confirms HS discriminability at BA-selected layers ($\Delta$AUROC +12--15pp; \cref{tab:ba_layer_results}) but cannot diagnose threshold-dependent bias.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Ensemble layer selection.} A three-layer ensemble averaging predictions from the precision-best, BA-best, and inverse-FPR-best layers bypasses single-metric selection. All four models show significant HS advantage ($p \leq 0.002$, HB; \cref{tab:ensemble}), including both 32B models that fail under CR-composite. The ensemble smooths metric-specific variation by averaging across depths within the deep region. This holdout result requires cross-validated confirmation (\cref{sec:discussion}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Cross-dataset replication (AdvBench).} We replicate the five-metric layer sweep on AdvBench~\citep{advbench} (520 traces, 159 jailbreaks, R1-8B, binary labels). The CR-composite selects shallow layers (median L2) while precision selects deep (median L14), a 31.7pp divergence (95\% CI [3.2, 48.4]pp, $p{=}0.015$; 1{,}000 bootstrap iterations) consistent with HarmThoughts. This confirms layer selection bias (C1) is geometric, not label-dependent; AdvBench does not validate C2 (\cref{app:advbench_layer_bias,app:advbench_cp}).\looseness=-1

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 & 384d & 3 & +17.5 & +20.1 & [+12.4, +28.3] & $-$7.9 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +5.8 & [$-$7.4, +19.5] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
OT-7B & L16 & 384d & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & [+14.0, +29.6] & $-$17.1 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+8.4, +29.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \checkmark \\[2pt]
QwQ$^\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 5 & +13.0 & +19.2 & [+9.3, +33.5] & $-$15.0 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$7.1, +27.7] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 1 & +18.7 & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & $-$14.6 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [$-$0.2, +24.3] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at threshold-based layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split, 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp); ``--'' = BA and FPR are not computed for the 1024d comparison because the 384d and 1024d encoders operate at different probe calibration points and do not share a decision threshold. HB: \checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$); all 384d model-level tests pass. $^\dagger$\,Sample-limited ($n \leq 20$); 32B results are exploratory. 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

The HS advantage is encoder-conditional: against BGE-large (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB correction (\cref{app:encoder_robustness}). Multi-seed stability appears in \cref{app:multi_seed}. Against the 384d baseline, HS probes outperform text probes in all four models$^\dagger$ (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}): cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B: +21.9pp precision, R1-8B: +20.1pp), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ (+19.2pp), and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (+15.4pp, pattern consistent with position mediation). R1-8B FPR is directionally consistent (5/5 seeds) but not robust ($p{=}0.044$, rank 12/12 in HB step-down; 1/5 seeds Bonferroni; \cref{app:multi_seed}). R1-32B passes 5/5 seeds but shows a pattern consistent with position mediation (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}); QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Overall, 18/20 model-seed combinations pass full HB (\cref{app:multi_seed}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 11.1--16.8pp above chance (\cref{app:control}). A split-half analysis on OT-7B confirms internal validity (\cref{app:split_half}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Capacity control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage across all four models$^\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$, all HB). The control provides partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. Random projection to 384d yields similar results ($\Delta$Prec +8.3 to +20.5pp; \cref{app:random_projection}), further weakening the capacity explanation. E5-Mistral-7B (4096d, PCA-reduced to 384d; \cref{app:e5_mistral}) yields intermediate results (2/4 HB), forming a monotonic encoder-quality gradient with MiniLM (4/4) and BGE-large (1/4).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} Against BGE-large~\citep{xiao2023cpack} (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB (1/4 precision). \cref{fig:forest_precision} visualizes the encoder-quality gradient: MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) $\to$ E5-Mistral (2/4) $\to$ BGE-1024d (1/4), with precision narrowing from +15--22pp to +6--18pp. The HS advantage is thus encoder-conditional; layer selection bias (C1) is encoder-independent.\looseness=-1

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_forest_precision.pdf}
\caption{HS precision advantage (pp) over text probes with 95\% bootstrap CI. Blue: vs.\ MiniLM-384d; orange: vs.\ BGE-1024d. Solid: survives HB; hollow: does not. The encoder-quality gradient is monotonic: all 384d comparisons significant; at 1024d only OT-7B survives.}
\label{fig:forest_precision}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Position mediation.} Position ($t/T$) accounts for 12--20pp of balanced accuracy, but all models retain non-positional signal above chance (all $p{<}0.001$; \cref{app:position_residual}). Under linear residualization, 3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (+3.7 to +6.9pp, HB); R1-32B is indistinguishable from zero (+1.9pp, below MDE). Quadratic residualization further absorbs the advantage in 2/4 models (QwQ-32B fully, R1-32B fully; R1-8B and OT-7B retain significance; \cref{tab:position_quadratic}). Temporal windowing reduces position dependence (\cref{sec:temporal}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Caveats.} Nested CV confirms the 7--8B advantage (10/10 folds, 13--25\% shrinkage; \cref{app:nested_cv}); the OT-7B holdout estimate is an upper bound (nested CV: L21--27 vs.\ holdout L16). R1-32B significance is limited to $W{=}1$ (25\% coverage); QwQ-32B lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps). FPR remains 11--50\%, precluding production deployment (\cref{sec:oracle}).\looseness=-1

\subsection{32B Scale}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

Results at 32B are exploratory$^\dagger$ ($n{=}19$--$20$; MDE 8.6--9.5pp; \cref{app:power_analysis}). Both models show divergent profiles---R1-32B suggests early commitment ($W{=}1$ only) while QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive---but neither meets the MDE threshold for confirmatory conclusions. Full analysis in \cref{app:32b_full}.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\cref{fig:window_ablation}): at 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp). GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:temporal_details}); an adaptive two-stage strategy offers marginal gains (\cref{app:two_stage}). CP-aligned probe trajectories confirm sharper HS transitions at the commitment point (\cref{fig:trajectory}).\looseness=-1

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_temporal_improvement.pdf}
\caption{Temporal window effect on R1-8B HS probe (L14). Precision: 69.3\% ($W{=}1$) to 82.2\% ($W{=}25$); FPR: 9.65\% to 4.83\%.}
\label{fig:window_ablation}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{CP-aligned probe score trajectories. Each panel averages all test traces for one model, with step 0 at the true commitment point. HS probes (blue, solid) show a sharper score increase at CP than text probes (orange, dashed), with OT-7B showing the strongest separation and R1-32B the weakest, consistent with the evidence gradient. Shaded bands: $\pm$1 std. Points with $<$5 traces are omitted.}
\label{fig:trajectory}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing (\cref{tab:negative}). BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian}/CUSUM fail (0\% detection on 3/4 models); Mahalanobis yields near-zero separation; RepE~\citep{zou2023representation} is model-dependent (BA 0.756 vs.\ 0.559); MLP probes overfit; CCS misaligns temporally; cross-model transfer fails (\cref{app:negative_details}). Per-model failure analysis (\cref{app:negative_details}) identifies mechanism-specific causes: BOCPD sensitivity to $\lambda$ prior, Mahalanobis collapse under high-dimensional HS, and CCS temporal misalignment. The signal is directional, gradual, model-specific, and linearly separable, excluding change-point, distance, transfer, and objective-alignment methods.\looseness=-1

